---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import IngressGenerator from '../../components/IngressGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Kubernetes Ingress Generator" description="Generate an Ingress resource YAML with host routing and optional TLS.">
  <IngressGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What Ingress does that a Service alone doesn't</h2>
  <p>A Kubernetes Service exposes a set of pods internally or via a basic load balancer, but Ingress adds HTTP-aware routing on top — hostname and path-based rules, and TLS termination — letting multiple applications share a single external entry point.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the hostname and backend service/port to route to.</li>
    <li>Enable TLS if you have a certificate configured.</li>
    <li>Apply the generated YAML with <code>kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>An Ingress routing <code>app.example.com</code> to a backend Service on port 80 lets external traffic reach your app through the cluster's configured Ingress controller (like nginx-ingress).</p>

  <h2>Common Ingress Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming Ingress works without an Ingress controller installed</strong> — the Ingress resource itself is just configuration; an actual controller (nginx, Traefik, etc.) must be running in the cluster to act on it.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting the referenced Service must already exist</strong> with matching name and port.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing annotations required by your specific Ingress controller</strong> for features like rewrite rules or rate limiting, which aren't part of the core Ingress spec.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Ingress Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Exposing a web application running in Kubernetes to external traffic</li>
    <li>Routing multiple hostnames to different backend services from one load balancer</li>
    <li>Terminating TLS at the cluster edge rather than in each application</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Kubernetes Ingress FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Do I need a separate Ingress controller?</strong> Yes — Ingress resources require a controller (nginx-ingress, Traefik, cloud-provider-specific ones) installed in the cluster to actually implement the routing rules.</p>
</ToolLayout>
